The assert keyword in Python provides a means of testing if a condition in your code returns True, if not, the program stops and throws an AssertionError. In other words, assertions are a way to check for false conditions and to provide feedback when they are not met, which can help you debug.Here’s how you would use assertions in your programs:Write assertions to check conditions that your code is supposed to handle. Normally, this kind of check would be performed elsewhere in your code. Any time a condition you have asserted should be True is found to be False, Python raises an AssertionError.
